(project borge-docs-engine
 (section format
  (doc "The file format")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Verbatim strings use open-pipe to open and pipe-close to close.
        The parser tracks nesting depth. Content between the opening
        delimiter and the final closing delimiter that returns depth
        to 0 is v_content. The closing delimiter is NOT included in
        v_content - it is a delimiter, not content. Nested delimiters
        inside the content ARE included verbatim.

        The inline directive: (inline filename.borg) declares that the
        named file is a sub-spec of this section. The file must exist and
        be parseable. The parent owns the architectural relationship;
        the child owns the detail. Inline is by filename, relative to the
        parent file.

        The no-inline declaration: (no-inline) in a project form means
        this project has no sub-specs. This is the legitimate standalone
        case. Every .borg file must either be inlined by a parent or
        declare no-inline. Any other .borg file is orphaned.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section verification
  (doc "Per-section acceptance criteria: how to know a feature works")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The (verify ...) stanza defines acceptance criteria for a section.
        It lives inside any (section ...) or (subsection ...) form.

        Syntax:
          (subsection login-page
            (status implemented)
            (verify
              (test "test/auth.ml")
              (build)
              (smoke "POST /api/login")
              (visual "/login")))

        Vocabulary is convention-defined:
          - ocaml-dune: build, test, smoke, visual, flow
          - go-standard: build, test, bench

        Immutability:
          Verify stanzas are part of the spec. Agents MAY NOT add,
          modify, or remove verify items. Only humans edit verify.
          borge lint --verify checks for invalid methods.
          borge drift detects verify stanza modifications.

        Execution model:
          borge does NOT run verification. It validates that:
          1. Methods are valid for the convention (lint)
          2. Verify stanzas are not modified by agents (drift)
          3. Implemented sections with verify have agent notes (drift)

        Agent workflow:
          When marking a section (status implemented), the agent reads
          the verify stanza, runs each check, and writes:
            (* agent note (|
              verified at commit abc123: build-pass, test-pass (22/22),
              smoke-pass (200 OK), visual-pass (login form visible)
            |) *)
          Verification claims are git-bound; freshness is computed
          from file hashes, not stored in .borg.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section spec-language-design
  (doc "Design principles for the borge spec language — declaration without evaluation")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The .borg format is a declaration language, not a programming
        language. A .borg file states what is true and what should be
        true. The tools verify whether reality matches the statement.
        The spec language does not evaluate, branch, loop, or mutate.
        It declares.

        This boundary — declaration without evaluation — is the
        defining architectural choice. Keeping it firm gives borge
        its most important properties:

        - Forward compatibility: unknown forms survive parsing
        - DSL extensibility: UI, DB, inbox coexist without parser changes
        - Human readability: the format stays small and learnable
        - Self-description: the grammar lives in borge.borg, not in types

        The risk of over-typing the core is that every new keyword
        form becomes a compiler change. Borge avoids this. The core
        Ast.sexp type stays generic. Typed consumption happens in
        consumer modules (spec.ml, ui_parse.ml, db_parse.ml) that
        interpret the generic tree.

        Where stronger types genuinely help is at the EXTRACTION
        boundary — the code that turns raw sexp into typed records.
        The current pattern is hand-rolled pattern matching repeated
        in every consumer. This is brittle: no exhaustiveness, silent
        skips, repeated logic.

        GADTs for extraction combinators (lib/core/extract.ml):
        A small combinator library with a GADT encoding "this extractor
        produces type 'a from a sexp". Example:

          val atom : string t
          val string : string t
          val keyword : string -> 'a t -> 'a t
          val ( let* ) : 'a t -> ('a -> 'b t) -> 'b t

        Then status extraction becomes:

          let status_extractor =
            let* st = keyword "status" atom in
            match status_of_string st with
            | Some s -> return s
            | None -> fail (`Invalid_status st)

        This stays entirely in OCaml's type system. The .borg format
        does not change. UI and DB DSLs define their own extractors
        on top of this core, replacing ~200 lines of hand-rolled
        pattern matching in ui_parse.ml and db_parse.ml with
        composable, testable declarations.

        Phantom types for the tool pipeline:
        Borge has a conceptual pipeline: balance -> parse -> lint
        -> drift -> review. A lightweight phantom type enforces this
        order:

          type balanced  type parsed  type linted
          type 'stage file = { ast : Ast.file; stage : 'stage }
          val balance : string -> balanced file result
          val parse : balanced file -> parsed file result
          val lint : parsed file -> linted file result

        Now lint cannot be called on a raw string. This is about
        the tools, not the spec language. It prevents real bugs
        with zero runtime cost.

        Richer constraint forms (without evaluation):
        The spec language can express more expectations without
        becoming executable. Declared constraints are verified by
        tools, not evaluated by the language:

          (depends-on STRING)
            -> resolved by borge future to build roadmaps
          (requires FORM)
            -> checked by borge lint
          (asserts FORM)
            -> verified by borge drift

        These are declarations of intent. The language does not
        resolve, check, or verify them. The tools do. This keeps
        .borg as a vocabulary for expectations while the typechecker
        of reality remains outside the language.

        Effects are not appropriate for borge:
        The current architecture is linear: read -> parse -> analyze
        -> produce findings. Effects shine with suspensible
        computations, plugin architectures, or complex resource
        management. Borge has none of these at scale. Effects
        would introduce non-local control flow into a simple
        linear pipeline, making it harder to follow.

        The one future place effects might fit is the LLM call
        layer (generate.ml, review_agent.ml). A perform (LLM_query
        prompt) effect with a configurable handler could cleanly
        separate "request evaluation" from "how that request is
        fulfilled" (real model, cache, timeout, mock). But even
        there, a functor-based interface is sufficient today.

        Self-describing validation:
        Since .borg files describe the .borg format in borge.borg
        itself, the tools could extract a schema from borge.borg
        and validate arbitrary .borg files against it. The spec
        describes the spec language. The tools validate conformity.
        This is the ultimate "types without evaluation": the
        language has no typechecker, but the tooling uses the
        self-description as a schema.
      |))
  )
  (subsection extract-combinators
   (doc "GADT-based extraction library for typed sexp consumers")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         lib/core/extract.ml: a GADT combinator library that replaces
         hand-rolled pattern matching in spec.ml, ui_parse.ml, and
         db_parse.ml.

         Types:
         - 'a t : an extractor that produces 'a from a sexp

         Primitives:
         - atom : string t
         - string : string t
         - int : int t
         - keyword : string -> 'a t -> 'a t
         - list : 'a t -> 'a list t
         - optional : 'a t -> 'a option t
         - record : fields -> 'a t

         Combinators:
         - ( let* ) : sequential composition
         - return : lift pure value into extractor
         - fail : raise structured error with context

         Usage in spec.ml:
         Replace the hand-rolled extract functions with combinator
         pipelines. The compiler checks exhaustiveness and type
         safety.

         Migration plan:
         1. Implement extract.ml with primitives and combinators
         2. Port spec.ml to use combinators (smallest surface)
         3. Verify edge case parity with existing tests
         4. Port ui_parse.ml and db_parse.ml
         5. Remove duplicated extraction helpers from each module
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection pipeline-phantoms
   (doc "Phantom types enforcing tool pipeline order at compile time")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Add phantom types to the file processing pipeline to
         prevent tools from being called on data at the wrong
         stage.

         Types:
         type balanced  type parsed  type linted  type checked
         type 'stage file = { ast : Ast.file; stage : 'stage }

         Functions (typed interface):
         Balance.balance : string -> balanced file result
         Parse.parse_file : string -> parsed file result
         Parse.from_balanced : balanced file -> parsed file result
         Lint.run : parsed file -> linted file result
         Check.run : linted file -> checked file result

         This is zero-cost: the 'stage type parameter is erased
         at runtime. The typechecker prevents bugs like calling
         lint on a raw filename string or on a file that bypassed
         balance. It documents the intended pipeline in types.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection richer-constraints
   (doc "Declares more expectations in .borg without making it executable")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Extend the language vocabulary with forms that declare
         expectations. These are not evaluated by the language;
         they are verified by the tools.

         (depends-on STRING)
           Declares that this section requires another section to
           be implemented first. Used by borge future to build
           dependency-aware roadmaps and detect ordering mistakes.

         (requires FORM)
           Declares invariants this section depends on.
           Examples: (requires (min-coverage 0.8))
                     (requires (max-complexity 12))
           Checked by borge lint. Violations produce findings.

         (asserts FORM)
           Declares expectations about code reality.
           Examples: (asserts (module-surface "drift.ml" exports 12))
                     (asserts (no-orphan-files))
           Verified by borge drift. Mismatches are drift findings.

         All three forms are parseable by the generic sexp parser.
         Spec.ml recognizes them as first-class metadata. The
         tools decide whether and how to enforce them.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection obligations
   (doc "Proof obligations — giving (asserts ...) a real checker")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (| Removed for lack of utility. The Coq proof experiment was implemented (emitter, runner, .borg.meta recorder, CLI verbs, lint rules) and live-discharged one property on the crud-app example, then removed after review. Findings: (1) the right tool for borge's invariants is types (typed operations, phantom resolved/unresolved refs), not Coq proofs over stringly-typed data — the ownership-consistency property was re-establishing typing discipline that strings threw away; (2) borge has no internal property that's both provable with reasonable effort and not already covered by an enforcement mechanism (lint checks code behavior; borge's invariants are about programs not data); (3) hand-authoring Coq witnesses is a specialist skill with no demand. The implementation lives in git history (commits b4f82f4, 6b40cb8, ad747c4, 1e35c29, bd4c89c). This section stays planned as a design reference if a real provable property emerges. |) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         richer-constraints (above) reserves (asserts FORM) as a
         declaration that "code reality matches an expectation," verified
         by borge drift. As written it has a gap: the only checkers drift
         can offer are structural (module-surface, orphan-files) or
         probabilistic (the LLM semantic review). Neither kind can make
         a claim about ALL inputs — only about some inputs the checker
         happened to look at.

         This subsection introduces the missing tier: a checker that
         discharges an obligation over the entire input space. Formally,
         that means a mechanical proof. Practically, the first backend is
         Coq (an interactive theorem prover), but the form is prover-
         agnostic: the .borg file only declares the obligation and points
         at a witness; an external tool runs and reports. The discipline
         rule is unchanged from the (verify ...) stanza in section
         verification: .borg declares; the tool discharges; borge records
         what was claimed and binds it to a commit. The .borg language
         STILL does not evaluate. Coq evaluates; borge records.

         Grammar — a property obligation inside any (section ...) or
         (subsection ...):

           (asserts
             (property NAME
               (statement (|...plain-English claim, human-readable...|))
               (witness PATH)               ; path to the proof file
               (prover coq)                 ; convention default may elide
               (severity error | warning)  ; default error
               (status proposed)))          ; elided => canonical; see Immutability

         Example (the first planned target — DB authorization soundness
         for the crud-app example, see examples/crud-app/db.borg):

           (subsection db-auth
             (asserts
               (property ownership-consistency
                 (statement (|
                   For every (group G) and operation op permitted on a
                   table T, either G is admin/can-all OR the table's
                   declared (ownership ...) column matches the column in
                   the group's (where ...) predicate. In particular, no
                   group is granted an operation whose ownership predicate
                   references a column the table does not declare owned.
                 |))
                 (witness "proof/db_auth.v")
                 (prover coq))))

         What each part means:
         - NAME: a stable identifier. The .borg.meta proof block and any
           inbox item or finding cite this name. Renaming a property is
           a spec change; the name is part of the contract.
         - statement: the human-readable claim. This is what a reader
           reads and what an agent quoting the property reproduces. It is
           NOT what the checker checks — the witness file is. The
           statement and the witness may drift; that drift is itself a
           finding (see meta block below).
         - witness: a path, repo-relative, to a file the prover can
           check. For Coq this is a .v file. The witness encodes the
           property formally and proves it against a representation of
           the spec/code reality that the proof tool emits. Borge never
           reads the witness file's contents; it only records the path
           and the prover's pass/fail verdict.
         - prover: which backend discharges this obligation. Initially
           only `coq`. The set of known provers is convention-defined
           and linted (see verify-methods in convention.ml). An unknown
           prover name is a lint error, not a silent skip.
         - severity: what happens if the prover fails. error blocks the
           commit (like a failing (verify (build))). warning surfaces a
           finding but does not block.

         Immutability (the agent-discipline boundary that matters):
           Obligations come in two states, and the rules differ per
           state. This mirrors how the rest of borge treats spec edits
           — Plan mode can author, Build mode cannot — but the bar for
           an obligation to become canonical is higher than for ordinary
           spec text, because obligations are the thing an agent is
           most tempted to rewrite when it is failing against one.

           PROPOSED obligations (Plan mode only):
             In Plan mode an agent MAY author a new (asserts
             (property ...)) stanza, but it MUST tag it:
               (asserts (property NAME (status proposed) ...))
             The (status proposed) tag is required on any agent-drafted
             obligation and is lint-enforced — an untagged
             agent-authored obligation is a lint error treated as a
             silent mutation regardless of mode. A proposed obligation
             is NOT discharged, does NOT contribute Verified credit,
             and does NOT block commits if a prover fails it. It is a
             draft for human review — the agent's suggestion of what
             property this section should carry, written in the spec
             language so a human can read and revise it in place.
           CANONICAL obligations (human-promoted only):
             A human reviews the proposed obligation, edits or approves
             it, and removes the (status proposed) tag. The tag's
             removal is the promotion act, and ONLY a human may perform
             it — promotion is the agent-discipline equivalent of Rule
             6 (agents write only agent comments). Once canonical, the
             obligation obeys the strict rule: agents MAY NOT modify
             or remove (asserts (property ...)) stanzas, the witness
             path, the prover, or the severity. Only humans edit them.
           Why the split exists — the failure mode this prevents:
             In a Ralph loop, an agent implementing a feature runs into
             a canonical property that its new code violates. The
             cheapest path for the agent is: rewrite the property to
             match what the code does. Proof re-discharges, loop
             terminates silently, the property is now weaker than the
             human intended and nobody found out it changed. That is
             exactly the failure mode borge exists to prevent, and it
             is already the rule for (verify ...) stanzas for the same
             reason. Canonical obligations are immutable to agents so
             this path is closed. The agent's recourse when a canonical
             property is wrong or unreachable is to file an inbox
             scope-concern (see docs/agent.borg inbox); a human reviews
             it and either fixes the property or tells the agent to
             live with it. The loop only terminates on a property a
             human wrote.
           Enforcement mirrors the existing (verify ...) rule: lint
           checks canonical obligation stanzas against git HEAD for
           agent-authored mutations; drift detects modifications; the
           commit lock refuses mutations by non-Plan modes. The
           (status proposed) tag is additionally lint-checked: an
           obligation with no (status ...) field is canonical and
           immutable to agents; an obligation with (status proposed)
           is malleable in Plan mode and inert (no discharge credit,
           no commit blocking) until promoted.

         What borge does NOT do:
           - Borge does not run the prover. Just as (verify (build)) is
             discharged by `dune build`, an obligation is discharged by
             invoking the prover against the witness + an emitted
             representation of the spec. That invocation is the
             borge-proof tool's job (a new lib/proof/ module, spec'd
             separately), not the .borg language's.
           - Borge does not generate the witness. Writing the proof is
             authorial work, like writing a test. borge may eventually
             scaffold a witness from the typed AST (e.g., emit a Coq
             module that represents the DB schema and the permission
             model), but the proof itself is human-authored.
           - Borge does not interpret the statement. Borge cannot tell
             whether the witness proves what the statement says. That
             is by design: the statement is for humans; the witness is
             for the machine. If they disagree, a reviewer reading both
             catches it. borge's job is to record that both exist and
             that the prover passed.

         The Verified status (already a value in lib/core/spec.ml's
         status ADT, currently unset by any tool):
           A section with status Verified must have at least one
           discharged CANONICAL obligation AND pass its convention's
           mandatory verify methods (build, test). Verified is not
           "no drift"; it is "drift is zero AND at least one property
           holds for all inputs AND was discharged at this commit."
           Lint enforces: (status verified) without a discharged
           canonical obligation is a lint error. An obligation whose
           witness no longer typechecks against the current spec/code
           representation downgrades the section's effective status to
           Implemented-at-best for report purposes, without rewriting
           the .borg status (the .borg is intent; the .borg.meta
           records the downgrade).

         Why "at least one" is the floor, not the ceiling:
           The Verified status as defined above is honestly weak: a
           section can earn it by discharging one tiny property ("the
           empty-list case is handled") while leaving the entire rest
           of its behavior unproven. This is a deliberate starting
           point — the lowest-friction bar at which the status means
           anything at all — but it is a compromise and reviewers
           should not read "Verified" as "substantially verified."
           To keep the status honest, reports show a count alongside
           it: (status verified) (properties 1) means one obligation
           discharged; a project dashboard surfaces verified/total per
           section so the reader can see at a glance which sections
           earned the status with one proof versus many. A per-section
           minimum may be set by a human (*.borg field, future work)
           to raise the bar above one where the cost is justified; the
           default minimum remains one so the floor is reachable.

         The admits cap:
           A canonical obligation discharged with admitted > 0 (see the
           admitted-count field below) does NOT block the commit —
           admits are a legitimate iterative proof-development
           workflow, exactly like skip-pending tests, and forbidding
           them would make iterative proof authorship impossible. But
           admits DO cap the status a section can earn: a section any
           of whose discharged obligations has admitted > 0 tops out
           at provisional-verified, NEVER verified, regardless of how
           many other clean obligations it carries. provisional-
           verified is reported separately in stats and dashboards so
           the distinction is visible, not buried. The cap cannot be
           removed by the agent (admits are inside the witness file,
           which agents cannot edit); it lifts automatically when the
           human finishes the proof and the prover re-runs with
           admitted: 0. This is the honesty mechanism that keeps
           proofs honest: admits don't fail the build, but they also
           can't silently count as full proofs.

         Evidence and freshness (same model as the (verify ...) agent
         note in section verification):
           When the prover is run and passes, borge writes:
             (* agent note (|
                proof-pass at commit abc123: ownership-consistency
                discharged (coq, 1 obligation, 0 admitted), stale=false
             |) *)
           Freshness is hash-derived from the witness file and the
           emitted representation, mirroring meta.borg staleness rules.
           A proof is stale when the spec/code it was checked against
           has changed since the prover ran; a stale proof contributes
           no Verified credit.

         .borg.meta block (new block type — see meta.borg future-blocks,
         which already invites additional blocks):

           (proof
             (commit abc123)
             (discharged-at 2026-07-04T18:00:00Z)
             (obligation ownership-consistency
               (witness "proof/db_auth.v")
               (prover coq)
               (verdict pass)
               (admitted 0)           ; count of admit/Admitted used
               (representation "proof/db_auth_schema.ml"))
               ; the emitted schema the witness was checked against
             (obligation ...
               (verdict fail)
               (failure "Theorem member_only_update: case g=member
                         not covered by ownership(owner-id).")))

         admitted count: a Coq proof may use `admit` to defer a
         sub-goal it cannot yet finish. From Coq's perspective the
         whole proof typechecks either way; the admit count is the
         only signal that distinguishes a complete proof from a pile
         of unproven assertions held together by trust. That is why
         it is recorded in the .borg.meta block and why it feeds the
         admits cap described above (provisional-verified, never
         Verified, while admits > 0). The count is extracted from
         the prover's output, not from the witness file's text —
         borge still does not read the witness; the prover reports
         the admit count as part of its verdict.

         Why this is the right first hard-checked tier:
           The crud-app DB authorization property is finite (closed
           schema of tables, columns, groups, can-rules), already
           encoded in the .borg (the groups/ownership/can declarations
           exist today), and is precisely what neither drift nor the
           LLM review can certify. drift catches presence; review
           catches prose-code mismatch; the proof catches a logical
           contradiction in the composition of two declarations in the
           same spec — the kind of bug that has no failing input a
           human would think to test, because it is a contradiction
           in the spec itself. Discharging one such property builds
           the entire obligations pipeline (spec form -> typed AST ->
           prover representation -> prover invocation -> meta block ->
           inbox finding on failure), which is reusable for every
           future (asserts (property ...)) — including the informal
           examples richer-constraints sketches, which become real the
           moment any one of them has a backend.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection effects-policy
   (doc "Why effects are deferred — and where they might fit later")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Effects are deferred because borge's architecture does not
         need them. The tool pipeline is linear and deterministic.
         Adding effects would make control flow implicit and harder
         to trace.

         Current position: do not use effects in borge.

         Future consideration: the LLM abstraction layer
         (generate.ml, review_agent.ml) may benefit from an effect
         that separates "request evaluation" from "how it is
         fulfilled" when testing and mocking become a burden.
         Until then, functor-based interfaces are sufficient.
      |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section config
  (doc "Project-level and user-level configuration for borge")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection config-file
   (doc ".borgerc: per-project configuration in sexp format")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Borge reads configuration from .borgerc files. Search order:
            1. Project root: .borgerc (checked into git, shared by team)
            2. User global: ~/.config/borge/config (not in git, personal)
          Project config overrides user config. If both define the same
          key, the project value wins.

          The file uses the same sexp format as .borg files.
          An empty or missing .borgerc means all defaults apply.

          Config file format:
          (borge-config
            (developer-name "roerick")
            (api-keys
              (anthropic (env ANTHROPIC_API_KEY))
              (openai (env OPENAI_API_KEY)))
          )

          Keys:
          - (developer-name STRING): the author name used for annotated
            comments. When set, borge make/plan/drift create comments
            with this authorship instead of "agent". When unset,
            defaults to "agent". This is the name that appears in
            (* developer-name note (|...|) *) comments.

          - (api-keys ...): map of provider names to API key sources.
            Each key is either:
              (PROVIDER (env ENV_VAR_NAME)) — read from environment
              (PROVIDER (value "literal-key")) — literal string (NOT
                recommended for checked-in files; prefer env refs)
            When borge needs to call an LLM directly (not via pi),
            it reads the relevant provider key from config. If not
            configured, borge falls back to pi or BORGE_AGENT_CMD
            as it does today.

          Why sexp format: consistency with the rest of borge.
          The parser already exists. No new syntax to learn.
          No TOML/YAML dependency. The config is a borge document.

          Why not just env vars: env vars work but don't carry
          developer-name or project-scoped settings. The config file
          gives a named, version-controlled, discoverable home for
          project preferences that env vars can't provide.

          Loading: lib/config.ml reads .borgerc, falls back to
          ~/.config/borge/config, merges project over user.
          The result is a typed config record available to all
          borge commands.
        |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section future-work
  (doc "Planned improvements and architectural decisions")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Parser rewrite: see sexp.borg (subsection sedlex-lexer) and
        (subsection menhir-parser). Moved there because the parser
        is a sexp library concern, not a borge top-level concern.

        Other possible future work:
        - Incremental parsing for editor integration
        - .borg language server protocol
        - Syntax highlighting definitions for common editors

        Turn-level drift checking (future integration):
        Borge currently checks spec/code alignment at the commit
        boundary — after the agent session ends, before the git commit.
        It does NOT check drift across turns within a session.
        Turn-level gating (rejecting an edit that violates the spec
        mid-session) is the role of a gate engine, which rein provides
        and borge does not yet integrate. When borge migrates from pi
        to rein, the loops-module primitives map onto rein's effects
        (call_llm, call_tool, checkpoint) and turn-level checking
        becomes available without restructuring.

        Rejected alternative — multi-agent orchestration:
        A multi-agent architecture (spawning a hierarchy of agents,
        each owning a subtree, coordinating via a manager) was
        considered and declined. Org structures exist primarily to
        manage human cognitive limits — specialization because one
        person cannot hold "great DBA" and "great security" in one
        head. A frontier model does not share that constraint the same
        way; role prompting ("you are an expert codemonkey") is context
        priming, not a capability ceiling. Building an architecture
        whose premise is a hierarchy of differently-specialized agents
        encodes a limitation the underlying system does not have, and
        the coordination overhead (merge, conflict, translation
        losses, a manager agent worse at managing than doing) becomes
        its own complexity sink. A single linear loop with boundary
        verification is simpler and more LLM-native. Subagents remain
        an occasional invocation-level tool (cheap model for a grep),
        not the execution backbone. Model selection at call time, not
        a permanent agent hierarchy.
      |))
  )
